  <tr>
    <td bgcolor="#FFFFFF">
	<div align=justify><font class="storytext"><P class=firstPara>by Emma Rigby Revolution UK 22 Aug 2005</P>
<P class=firstPara>LONDON - <STRONG>'Revolver',</STRONG> the underworld gambling flick from British director<STRONG> Guy Ritchie, whose wife Madonna is at home recovering after a horse-riding accident, will be supported online in an extensive push.</STRONG></P>
<P><STRONG>Agency Greenroom Digital has won a contract to develop a digital campaign</STRONG>, which will include interactive advertising, viral videos, and a website to launch in <STRONG>September 2005.</STRONG></P>
<P>The Sun Online is a partner in the campaign, and is running a trailer for 'Revolver' on its site.</P>
<P>Victoria Keeble, head of film at Greenroom, said: "<STRONG>We want the digital campaign to be as exciting as the film itself -- we're looking at new and innovative ways of reaching the audience, including downloads, viral and interactive content</STRONG>."</P>
<P>Director Ritchie is reunited with 'Lock Stock and Smoking Barrels' actor <STRONG>Jason Statham</STRONG>, who plays a hotshot gambler in the movie. But when he beats casino boss and crime lord <STRONG>Dorothy Macha</STRONG>, things get serious.</P>
<P><STRONG>Ritchie is reported to be looking for a new star to add glamour to the movie premiere after his singer wife cracked three ribs</STRONG> and broke her collar bone and a hand in a riding accident at her 1,200-acre English country estate, Ashcombe House. She was celebrating her 47th birthday.</P>
</font></div>
